Let $R$ mean red and $B$ mean blue. After a red counter is drawn, $5$ red and $3$ blue counters remain: $8$ counters altogether. After a blue counter is drawn, $6$ red and $2$ blue counters remain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=1cm,y=1cm]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2.6,1.6);
\coordinate (B) at (2.6,-1.6);
\coordinate (RR) at (5.6,2.7);
\coordinate (RB) at (5.6,0.6);
\coordinate (BR) at (5.6,-0.6);
\coordinate (BB) at (5.6,-2.7);
\draw (O)--(R) node[midway,above,fill=white] {$\frac{6}{9}$};
\draw (O)--(B) node[midway,below,fill=white] {$\frac{3}{9}$};
\draw (R)--(RR) node[midway,above,fill=white] {$\frac{5}{8}$};
\draw (R)--(RB) node[midway,below,fill=white] {$\frac{3}{8}$};
\draw (B)--(BR) node[midway,above,fill=white] {$\frac{6}{8}$};
\draw (B)--(BB) node[midway,below,fill=white] {$\frac{2}{8}$};
\path (R) node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] {$R$};
\path (B) node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] {$B$};
\path (RR) node[right] {$R\quad RR$};
\path (RB) node[right] {$B\quad RB$};
\path (BR) node[right] {$R\quad BR$};
\path (BB) node[right] {$B\quad BB$};
\path (2.6,3.5) node {Draw 1};
\path (5.6,3.5) node {Draw 2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Every second-stage denominator falls from $9$ to $8$. Only the colour drawn loses one from its count.
